nnie mae and freddie mac  didn't have the you know the spine to go  after that second of all they did  nothing about too big to fail now you  know Dodd and Frank will say oh yes we  did we've put together these um what do  they call resolution authority there's  the resolution authority that's going to  allow a  unwind of a threatening institution if  it's too big and too large and too  politically are interconnected or if  it's on the precipice we will unwind  this through the resolution authority  but the problem with the resolution  authority is that it requires the  committee the F sock it's called to vote  to in fact resolve and unwind the  institution and I think that that is a  step that many many people would not  choose to take it would again be a  moment just as the moments that faced  Geithner and Paulson in 2008 which was  it's easier just to bail it out it's  easier just to make the taxpayers pay  and then deal with it later than it is  to make the hard decisions to let a firm  get unwound and these are very large and  powerful firms that have a lot of  friends in Washington so I I am just  very dubious that the resolution  authority is going to work as well as  Dodd and Frank seemed to think that it  will in resolving large interconnected  institutions so I think there were a lot  of failures there were some good things  about dodd-frank but for the 2,000 pages  of whatever you know I just don't think  it's put us that much closer to a period  where the taxpayers can sleep at night  without worrying about having to bail  out companies in the future yes  thank you very much my name is Tyler  O'Neill recent Hillsdale graduate and  aspiring journalists oh really good for  you jump one that's the idea okay my  question is regarding the idea of  regulations and holding these businesses  accountable President Wilson famously  argued that the economy had become so  complicated that we needed a bigger  government to rein in excesses and yet  we see a movement 